<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">An amoeba is a tree together with instructions how to iteratively grow trees by adding paths of a fixed length <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
This paper analyses such a growth process.
An amoeba is mortal if all versions of the process are finite,
and it is immortal if they are all infinite.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We obtain some necessary and some sufficient conditions for mortality.
In particular, for growing caterpillars in the case <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1</annotation></semantics></math> we characterize mortal amoebas.
We discuss variations of the mortality concept, conjecture that some of them are equivalent, and support this conjecture for <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> tree, amoeba, caterpillar, mortality</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A classical result of Dirac from 1961
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>
states that every (non-null) chordal graph has a vertex whose neighborhood is a clique.
This result was generalized in the literature in various ways <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
In 2019, Beisegel et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> proposed a common generalization of all these results by introducing the concept of <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">avoidable paths</em>.
An induced path <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">avoidable</em> if every extension of <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in an induced cycle.
Here, an <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extension</em> of <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is any induced path in <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that can be obtained by extending <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> by one edge from each endpoint.
Beisegel et al. conjectured that for every positive integer <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, every graph that contains an induced <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex path also contains an avoidable induced <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex path, and proved the statement for the case <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The general conjecture was proved in 2020 by Bonamy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
A further strengthening was given by Gurvich et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, who showed that in every graph, every induced path can be transformed into an avoidable one via a sequence of shifts (where two induced <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex paths are said to be <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shifts</em> of each other if their union is an induced path with <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> the concept of avoidability was applied to general graphs rather than only paths.
A graph <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with roots <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t\in V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t\in V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inherent</em> if every graph <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that contains an induced copy of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, also contains an avoidable induced copy of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the special case when <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(v_{1},\dots,v_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(v_{1},\dots,v_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a path with <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the result of Bonamy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> states that <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is inherent.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> the authors develop a so-called pendant extension method for showing that a fixed <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-rooted graph is not inherent, and identify many such <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-rooted graphs.
This method is based on the process of growing trees.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          The case of trees is the only non-trivial case: using the pendant extension method it is easily shown that the process stops for all connected graphs which contain a cycle (see also <a href="#Thmremark2" title="Remark 2. ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Remark</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</span></span></span>
If this process halts, a particular <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-rooted tree is non-inherent.
It is open if the halting problem for this process is decidable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above process is of independent interest.
In this paper we generalize it in two ways. First, we allow arbitrarily many roots. Second, a step of our process may add an arbitrary path to a root, rather than a single edge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An amoeba is a tree with a number of roots assigned to each vertex.
A tree <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> may have several copies of a particular amoeba.
Each one requires a ‘certain growth space’ in <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. If it cannot grow within <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the amoeba will live on by expanding <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> – attaching new paths at the points of its roots.
In this way new copies of the amoeba may appear in the expanded <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Alternatively, if every copy of the amoeba in <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has enough growth space, the <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> will not grow anymore.
We say that <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> confines such an amoeba.
To specify the finiteness of growth of a given amoeba we introduce several notions of <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mortality</em>.
In this paper we present some necessary and some sufficient conditions for amoeba mortality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our process of growing trees has relations to several known models:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">String and graph generation models </span> are intensively studied in theoretical computer science, formal language theory, and mathematical biology.
There are many models of this sort: cellular automata, rewriting systems, graph grammars, etc. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. In particular,
our model resembles graph relabelling systems with forbidden contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Most graph generation models use labelling of graphs. In contrast, we propose a simple rule of tree growth governed by the structure of subtrees and does not use labels on vertices and/or edges. The resulting process is interesting from the graph-theoretical point of view and possibly might impact the area of graph generation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Galton-Watson process </span> is a probabilistic process of growing trees.
It is a foundational concept in the realm of probability theory, specifically in the study of family trees and the dynamics of progeny within populations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Introduced by Francis Galton and Henry Watson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, this stochastic process serves as a mathematical model to examine the transmission of traits, characteristics, or lineage through successive generations of families <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. The key idea revolves around the probability distribution of offspring within a family, where individuals independently give rise to a random number of descendants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This model has proven particularly valuable in fields such as genetics and demography <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, providing insights into the likelihood of lineage continuation or extinction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. The Galton-Watson process remains an essential tool for understanding the unpredictable nature of family structures and the variability in the number of progeny across generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the Galton-Watson process, the replication criteria of the amoeba replication process is not dictated by a probability distribution. Instead, the expansion is orchestrated by the existence of a specific subgraph within the existing tree, acting as a trigger for the generation of additional leaves or progeny.
This departure from stochastic randomness to a combinatorial criterion opens up new avenues for exploring the interplay between structure and growth of trees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is structured as follows. In the beginning of <a href="#S2" title="2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we introduce the main concepts of the paper, such as <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">amoeba</em>, its <em id="S1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extension</em>, <em id="S1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">growth</em>, <em id="S1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mortality</em>, <em id="S1.p9.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">immortality</em>, and <em id="S1.p9.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confinement</em>.
In <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we relate mortality and confinement conditions, while in <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Orbit lemma ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we discuss relations between the symmetries of the underlying graphs and amoeba mortality.
In <a href="#S3" title="3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we focus on the mortality conditions for amoebas growing in the simplest way, namely, by attaching pendant edges.
We identify degree conditions which are necessary for any such amoeba to be not mortal in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Degree conditions ‣ 3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, while in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Mortality criteria for caterpillars ‣ 3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> we describe the not mortal amoebas whose underlying graph is a caterpillar.
We conclude the paper with <a href="#S4" title="4 Directions for future work ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, in which we propose some conjectures as well as directions for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Amoebas</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">amoeba</em><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          This name was recently given to a different class of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. We think that these two classes cannot be confused.</span></span></span> <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(T,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(T,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a pair consisting of a tree <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the
<em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">root function</em>
<math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:V(T)\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:V(T)\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping every vertex <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to a non-negative integer <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> called the <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiplicity</em> of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A vertex from <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of positive multiplicity is called a root of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, we define <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the number of roots</em>
of <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V(H)}m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V(H)}m(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set of roots of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supp(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supp(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-extension of amoeba</em> <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by applying to each vertex <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> the following procedure: take <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint paths
<math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and identify one endpoint of each with <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. The paths are called <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extension paths</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">copy of amoeba</em> <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an amoeba <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{\prime},m^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{\prime},m^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}\leq G</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\cong H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\cong H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the functions <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide under the corresponding isomorphism.
When there is no possibility of ambiguity, we denote an amoeba and its copy by the same notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
be a copy of an amoeba in a tree <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then an <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth</em> <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree
containing both <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-extension of <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and having the minimal possible number of edges.
Note that an <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth is not uniquely defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If, for a copy <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of an amoeba in graph <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, all growths of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> coincide with <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then the copy is called <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dead</em>. Otherwise, the copy is called <em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alive</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a copy of an amoeba may have non-isomorphic growths; see an
example in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.07484v2/growth-10-mps.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="width:12.041em; height:6.413em;aspect-ratio:160/85;" width="160" height="85"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.07484v2/growth-11-mps.svg" id="S2.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="width:12.041em; height:6.413em;aspect-ratio:160/85;" width="160" height="85"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Non-isomorhic 3-growths of the same copy of an amoeba. The copy
is shadowed in grey, the new edge is dashed</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For an amoeba <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">growth sequence</em> of <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> starting in a tree <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
is a (possibly infinite) sequence of trees <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0},T_{1},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0},T_{1},\dots</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}=T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth of a copy of <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i+1}\neq T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i+1}\neq T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence is <em id="S2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximal</em> if it cannot be extended.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It will be useful to define the following special case of maximal <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequences:
for an amoeba <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generation sequence</em> of <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence starting in <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. See an example in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.07484v2/growth-3-mps.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:28.453em; height:7.113em;aspect-ratio:378/94;" width="378" height="94"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A generation sequence for an amoeba <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth of <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from the context it will be omitted in notation for extensions, growths, generation sequences, etc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> be the extension parameter, let <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an amoeba, and let <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree. We introduce the following definitions.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Amoeba <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">immortal</em> w.r.t <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> if all maximal <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequences of <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> starting in <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are infinite.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Amoeba <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mortal</em> w.r.t <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> if all maximal <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequences of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> starting in <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are finite.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is (im)mortal w.r.t <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is (im)mortal.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A tree <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confining</em> for an amoeba <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> if it admits a copy of <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and all copies of <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are dead.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">Note that any <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence starting in a confining tree is of length <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains open whether all generation sequences of <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> can be infinite.</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An amoeba is <em id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">totally confined</em> if for each tree <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> containing a copy of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a confining tree <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> containing a copy of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confining trees can be replaced by confining
graphs.
Totally confined amoebas can be defined in this more general setting too.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can use general graphs rather than trees in the extension process (in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> it was done for general two-rooted graphs).
Since attaching paths to a graph does not change its set of cycles, this process is finite for non-trees.
As we mainly study mortality, we restrict our attention to the case of trees.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Initial observations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a copy of an amoeba may have non-isomorphic growths, which lead to essentially different growth sequences.
We are going to show that this effect can take place only for early stages of the growth process and only for amoebas that are small enough w.r.t the growth parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
An example in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> cannot be seen in generation sequences, as we will prove. It is caused by the minimality condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth of an alive copy of an amoeba <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> in a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding extension of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a path <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> in the extension starting in a vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}\in V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}\in V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell}\}\notin E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell}\}\notin E(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Take a connected component of the forest <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}-H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}-H</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is called an <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">area</em> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the minimality condition, the depth of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (the maximal length of a path from the root to a leaf) is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unique path of length <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> from the root <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that other edges in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges of the original tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way we come to the following conclusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The intersection of an area of an extension path and the original graph is a tree of depth at most <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and its subtree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a partial order on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u\leq^{T^{\prime}}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leq^{T^{\prime}}v</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the path from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{u:v\leq^{T^{\prime}}u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u:v\leq^{T^{\prime}}u\}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will not mention <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the exponent when it is clear from the context.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0},T_{1},\dots,T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0},T_{1},\dots,T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence for an amoeba <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(T_{k})\setminus V(T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(T_{k})\setminus V(T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> has depth at most <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If for some vertex <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> there are two different paths <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of equal lengths from <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to a leaf, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{{diam}}(H)\leq 2\ell-4\ \text{and}\ \text{{diam}}(T_{0})\leq 2\ell-6." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>diam</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>diam</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{{diam}}(H)\leq 2\ell-4\ \text{and}\ \text{{diam}}(T_{0})\leq 2\ell-6.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">W.l.o.g. we can assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have no common edges.
Indeed, otherwise we can change the vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> by the last common vertex of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choose the maximal <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that at least one edge of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cup P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cup P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
W.l.o.g. we can assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(u_{0},u_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(u_{0},u_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the last edge of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It means that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is added in a growth in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of a copy <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> of the amoeba.
So an extension of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contains an extension path from a root <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not on the path from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that has no common edges with <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, otherwise <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the extension path can be changed to the path containing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the minimality condition; see Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a).
Also, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is not on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, otherwise the extension should add more edges, since the length of the whole path <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>; see Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b).</span></p>
</div>
